  "step_1": "The decision reason is based on comparing the skewness of the two lotteries, specifically preferring the one that is more right-skewed (i.e., assigns higher probability to unfavorable outcomes relative to favorable ones). In this context, 'unfavorable outcomes' are negative monetary outcomes (losses), and 'favorable outcomes' are zero or positive outcomes. Both lotteries only involve zero or negative outcomes, so the only unfavorable non-zero outcomes are the losses: -6000 in Lottery B and -3000 in Lottery A. Lottery A has a 0.2% chance of -3000, while Lottery B has a 0.1% chance of -6000. Since Lottery A has a higher probability (0.2% vs 0.1%) of a loss (albeit a smaller loss), it is more right-skewed than Lottery B. Therefore, the decision reason would prefer Lottery A because it is more right-skewed. The relevant information (probabilities and outcomes) is available to assess skewness, so the reason can be applied.",
  "step_2": "The individual describes the decision as close ('Knappe Entscheidung'), indicating some deliberation. The key consideration expressed is that the prospect of losing 6000 euros ('die Aussicht auf die -6000') is very unattractive ('sehr unatraktiv'). This suggests the individual focused on the magnitude of the potential loss in Lottery B and found it particularly aversive. The individual does not mention probabilities, comparisons of likelihoods, or structural features like skewness. The justification centers on the emotional or evaluative response to the larger loss, not on distributional properties of the lottery. There is no indication that the individual considered how probabilities are distributed across outcomes or which lottery is more right-skewed.",
  "step_3": "The decision reason requires evaluating and preferring the more right-skewed lottery (Lottery A, which has higher probability of a loss). However, the individual’s reasoning focuses solely on the unattractiveness of the larger loss in Lottery B, without any reference to probabilities or skewness. The individual may be relying on a 'minimax regret' or 'loss aversion' heuristic, avoiding the option with the worst possible outcome. While both lotteries involve losses, the reason’s focus on skewness (probability distribution) is absent in the verbal report. The justification is outcome-based (magnitude of loss), not distribution-based. Therefore, there is no evidence that the individual used skewness as a decision criterion. The information considered (large loss aversiveness) does not match the reason’s required information (probability distribution of unfavorable outcomes), and the justification (avoid big loss) does not align with the reason’s rule (prefer more right-skewed lottery).",
  "final_assessment_score": 10
